      "chapters": []
    },
    {
      "id": "pKIhvnGm12U",
      "title": "Bedrule Church, Scottish Borders",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-14T13:13:05Z",
      "durationSec": 291,
      "thumb": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/pKIhvnGm12U/mqdefault.jpg",
      "description": "Bedrule Church replaced an earlier chapel and was paid for by the Usher family. The church contains part of a Viking hog-back stone and many shields bearing the coats of arms of local families. The Caln Comyn once held these lands and later they passed to the Douglas Clan. The motto of 'I Saved the King' belongs to the Turnbulls, one of whom supposedly saved Robert the Bruce from a wild bull. The church nows belongs to the community.",
      "chapters": []
    },
    {
      "id": "k_Xu6kBxE5s",
      "title": "Legerwood Kirk, 1127, Scottish Borders",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-10T20:33:28Z",
      "durationSec": 288,
      "thumb": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/k_Xu6kBxE5s/mqdefault.jpg",
      "description": "Legerwood Kirk is one of Scotland's oldest churches and is located near Jedburgh in the Scottish Borders. The Reverend Calderwood was a Covenanter and after losing his church he held illegal conventicles for his parishoners. Grizzel Cochrane is buried here with her husband John Kerr of Morriston. She is remembered for saving her father from execution by taking the death warrant from the courier, dressed as a highywayman.",
      "chapters": []
    },
    {
      "id": "FOgtyT36h3s",
      "title": "St Cattan's Kirk and Mausoleums, Aberuthven, Perth and Kinross",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-03T11:58:19Z",
      "durationSec": 201,
      "thumb": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/FOgtyT36h3s/mqdefault.jpg",
      "description": "St Cattan's is one of Scotland's oldest Christian sites. The ruins date from the 13th century and it ceased to be a parish church in  1673. Two of the mausoleums have been built within the old kirk and the old laird's loft was converted in 1736 by John Adam to become the burial site of the Graham's who became the Dukes of Montrose. The parents of the 'Great Montrose,' James Graham are said to be buried here.",
      "chapters": []
    },
    {
      "id": "9V2hLFH0ulk",
      "title": "Johnstone Railway Station, Renfrewshire, Ayrshire Coast Line.",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-02T13:48:34Z",
      "durationSec": 165,
      "thumb": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/9V2hLFH0ulk/mqdefault.jpg",
      "description": "Johnstone railway station was opened in 1840 by the Glasgow, Paisley, Kilmarnock and Ayr Railway. It was known as Johnstone High for a few years. A busy station with regular services. A large goods yard was once located here and one of the sidings served a basin on the Glasgow, Paisley and Ayr Canal. Cars from the Linwood Car Plant were once loaded on to trains nearby.",
      "chapters": []
    },
    {
      "id": "aEuGjPCxxq0",
      "title": "The Wester Pickston Railway near Methven in Perth and Kinross",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-01T09:20:07Z",
      "durationSec": 410,
      "thumb": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/aEuGjPCxxq0/mqdefault.jpg",
      "description": "The Scottish Model Engineering Trust moved here from Perth in 2002 and have built a 2 km long line with 7.25 and 5 inch mixed gauge track. On the two loops are Geoff's Halt, Duck End Station and the main sttaion at Pickston. The line has electric light signalling. Steam and diesel locomotives are in use.",
      "chapters": []
    },
    {
      "id": "oISQgzXNro0",
      "title": "The Lathalmond Railway Museum, Dunfermline",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-09T21:27:11Z",
      "durationSec": 253,
      "thumb": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/oISQgzXNro0/mqdefault.jpg",
      "description": "The Lathalmond Railway Museum has been built on the site of the old WWII Royal Navy Stores Depot. The standard gauge line is quite short at the moment. A small station has been built and a signal box. The old loco shed is still in use. Hunslet and Andrew Barclay diesel locomotives are in use.",
      "chapters": []
    },
    {
      "id": "XEElSPPA-As",
      "title": "Milliken Park railway station, Ayrshire Coast Line, Johnstone",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-09T09:40:34Z",
      "durationSec": 199,
      "thumb": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/XEElSPPA-As/mqdefault.jpg",
      "description": "Milliken Park is a station dating from 1989 that replaced the old station closed in 1966 that lay slightly closer to Johnstone. The next station\ntowards Ayr is Howwood. Trains call generally every 30 mins. Unusually\nno car park is provided, however a large housing estate is close by. The name 'Cochrane Mill' was first used for the 1840 station, however it\nbecame 'Milliken' in 1853 after the businessman who purchased the land.",
      "chapters": []
